title: Victory at Dachau
subtitle: The Red Army beats Bamberg's troops. Noske sends his own.
new-page: true
tags: event
view-if: crisis_bavaria = 1 and crisis_time = 2
max-visits: 1

= Victory at Dachau

North of Munich, on 16 April, the Red Army met the troops Hoffmann had scraped together. Ernst Toller, who commands on that front, had not wanted to fight. His men fought anyway and took Dachau. Bamberg's volunteers ran.

It may be the only victory the council republic ever wins. Hoffmann has swallowed his Bavarian pride and asked Berlin for help. Noske is sending General von Oven with Prussian and Württemberg troops and the Freikorps of Epp and Oberland, more than thirty thousand men with artillery and aircraft. The Red Army has perhaps fifteen thousand. [? if bav_power = 1 : In Munich, the Executive Council has its back to the wall and its hand on the factories. ?][? if bav_power = 2 : In Munich, the councils' executive argues while our party fights in the ranks. ?][? if bav_power = 3 : In Munich, the coalition executive holds together, just. ?]

- @advance: Advance north and carry the revolution to Augsburg and Regensburg.
- @dig_in: Dig in around Munich and train the army.
- @truce: Let Toller offer Bamberg a truce.

@advance
subtitle: The Left's answer: a revolution that stands still is lost.
